Opportunity Analysis: Tokenized Treasury/Cash Management OS
1. Background (2024–2025 Developments Driving the Opportunity)
The convergence of high interest rates, real-world asset (RWA) tokenization, and institutional crypto adoption in 2024–2025 has set the stage for a Tokenized Treasury/Cash Management OS. After a decade of near-zero yields, global interest rates climbed above 5% in 2023–2024, making money market funds (MMFs) far more attractive than idle bank depositscalastone.com. This yield environment has spurred a boom in tokenized U.S. Treasury funds on blockchain. Notably, BlackRock launched its first tokenized money market fund BUIDL in March 2024, which rapidly scaled to $1+ billion AUM by early 2025 and exceeded $2 billion by mid-2025ccn.comccn.com. Franklin Templeton’s “Benji” on-chain U.S. Government Money Fund (ticker BENJI) also expanded across multiple networks (Stellar, Polygon, Aptos, Base, Avalanche, Arbitrum, and Ethereum) in 2024blockworks.co. By late 2024 Franklin’s on-chain fund reached $409 million AUM (making it the third-largest tokenized fund after BlackRock’s BUIDL and Ondo’s offering)blockworks.co. These initiatives, along with others (e.g. VanEck’s VBILL fund and planned products from Galaxy Digitalca.finance.yahoo.comphemex.com), demonstrate a new supply of regulated, yield-bearing tokens entering the market.
Intraday yield and 24/7 liquidity have also emerged as game-changers. In mid-2025 Franklin Templeton introduced a patent-pending “intraday yield” feature for BENJI that accrues interest “down to the second” of holdingthedefiant.io. An investor can hold BENJI tokens for even a few hours and still earn proportional yield for that partial day, with daily interest distribution including weekendsthedefiant.io. This precise accrual was previously impractical in traditional funds, and it underscores a new demand for maximizing yield on idle cash even for very short durations. In parallel, blockchain’s always-on nature enables near-instant settlement and 24/7 liquidity, addressing legacy limitations where fund transactions only processed during bank hours. For example, industry leaders note that stablecoins still depend on traditional banking hours for mint/redemptions, whereas a fully tokenized fund can allow 24/7 creation, redemption, and even repo transactionscalastone.comcalastone.com. Indeed, the passage of the U.S. GENIUS Act in July 2025 (establishing a regulatory framework for stablecoins) has further bridged TradFi and DeFi. The Act permits stablecoin issuers to hold reserves in registered government money market fundsdechert.com, encouraging overlap between stablecoin liquidity and MMFs. It also bans stablecoin issuers from paying interest to holdersdechert.com, which paradoxically increases the incentive for treasurers to seek yield via their own investments in T-bill funds (since stablecoins themselves cannot pass yield through).
BlackRock’s BUIDL tokenized money market fund rapidly surpassed $2 billion in assets by mid-2025, exemplifying the surge of institutional interest in on-chain Treasuriesccn.com. BUIDL (managed by BlackRock with Securitize as transfer agent) pays daily interest and operates across multiple blockchains, signaling a major shift toward blockchain-based cash managementccn.comccn.com.
In summary, 2024–2025 brought three pivotal changes: (a) Tokenized T-bill funds went live at scale, led by brand-name asset managers and hitting multi-billion AUM within monthsccn.com; (b) Demand for intraday yield and continuous deployment of idle liquidity became a reality, with technology enabling interest accrual for every second assets are heldthedefiant.iothedefiant.io; and (c) Regulatory greenlights (like the GENIUS Act) began to legitimize stablecoin and tokenized fund usage in institutional contextscalastone.comdechert.com. These shifts have created fertile ground – and market urgency – for an integrated treasury management solution tailored to tokenized cash instruments.
2. Prior State of Treasury Management vs. New Needs Today
Before these developments, treasurers had limited options to earn yield on digital assets, and those options often fell short. The default approach for crypto-native firms’ on-chain treasuries was to hold stablecoins (USDC, USDT, DAI), treating them as “digital cash”backed.fibacked.fi. Stablecoins provided stability and liquidity but earned 0% yield (Circle’s USDC, for instance, pays no interest)backed.fi. Some companies did deploy stablecoins into CeFi lenders or DeFi protocols in search of interest – but this introduced significant counterparty and smart contract risk, as painfully demonstrated by 2022’s lender failures. Moreover, on-chain “low-risk” yields (e.g. lending USDC on Aave) typically hovered around 1–2%, far below the ~5% risk-free rates available on U.S. T-bills off-chainbacked.fi. In effect, crypto treasurers were stuck choosing between safety and yield: holding stablecoins kept principal safe but forfeited interest, while chasing yield meant assuming new risks. Traditional corporate treasurers, on the other hand, simply left surplus cash in bank deposits or conventional money market funds – but those funds settle on T+1 basis or require moving money in and out of banking channels, making them incompatible with real-time crypto transactions. Prior to 2024 there was effectively no seamless way to keep working capital on-chain and earning a competitive yield.
This is a new need born of recent changes. With short-term rates now so high, the opportunity cost of sitting in non-yielding stablecoins or uninsured exchange accounts is hard to ignore. Crypto firms learned this firsthand in 2023 when USDC’s temporary bank-run “depeg” (due to a custodial bank failure) showed that even trusted stablecoins carry idiosyncratic riskbacked.fibacked.fi. As one RWA platform noted, relying on a single stablecoin or bank exposes treasuries to single points of failure and inflation risk, without adequate returnbacked.fibacked.fi. Now that tokenized U.S. Treasury funds are available, treasurers have a way to diversify into on-chain T-bills that generate 4–5% yield, improving both safety (backed by real Treasuries held off the bank balance sheetbacked.fi) and returnsbacked.fi. There is also a liquidity management angle: intraday and overnight cash no longer needs to sit idle. For example, a company could park spare stablecoin balances into a tokenized MMF each night or even for a few hours and earn interest “for the exact seconds” the assets were heldcalastone.comthedefiant.io. This was impractical before tokenization – traditional funds didn’t credit partial-day holdings – but intraday yield features now make it feasiblethedefiant.iothedefiant.io.
In short, what’s changed is that treasurers (both crypto-native and traditional) can finally have their cake and eat it too: keep liquidity in a blockchain-ready form without sacrificing yield. The new breed of tokenized T-bill funds bridges the gap between stablecoin-like liquidity and money-market-like returns. This creates a need for tools to actually utilize these instruments in day-to-day treasury operations. Previously, no one asked for a “tokenized treasury OS” because the tokens and use-cases didn’t exist. Now they do – and companies will demand better ways to manage these new assets.
3. Gaps in Current Capabilities and Pain Points
Despite the rapid rise of tokenized cash products, the supporting software and infrastructure for treasurers remain immature. Early adopters face several capability gaps in 2025:
	• Fragmented Wallet & Chain Management: Unlike a bank account (one portal for all funds), on-chain assets live in disparate wallets and networks. BlackRock’s BUIDL, for instance, operates on Ethereum, Solana, Polygon, Arbitrum, Optimism, Avalanche, and Aptosccn.comccn.com. An institution might hold BUIDL tokens on multiple chains to optimize for liquidity or fees, and also hold other fund tokens on different networks. Today there is no unified dashboard to view and control all these holdings across chains. Users must manually track multiple addresses or rely on custodian platforms that are not tailored for this purpose. A tokenized treasury OS should provide a single pane of glass to manage assets across all supported blockchains, abstracting away the complexity of bridges and network fragmentation (BUIDL currently uses Wormhole bridge to move tokens between chainsccn.com, but the end-user experience of using that is not trivial).
	• Subscription, Redemption, and Payment Workflows: Acquiring or redeeming tokenized fund shares is more involved than trading a typical crypto token. These funds usually require investor whitelisting (KYC/qualification via the transfer agent) and use off-chain settlement for final cash movements. For example, to buy BUIDL one must be verified by Securitize and typically use USDC as the subscription currencyccn.com; redemptions historically paid out via bank wires in USD after a delayccn.comccn.com. Such processes are not yet automated or integrated into any treasury software – they often involve emails, portals, or manual blockchain transactions. Recent innovations are starting to close this gap: Securitize (the transfer agent for BUIDL and other funds) just enabled a smart-contract based off-ramp where holders of BlackRock’s BUIDL or VanEck’s VBILL fund can instantly swap their tokenized shares for Ripple’s USD stablecoin (RLUSD) 24/7ccn.comccn.com. This is a big improvement over waiting T+2 days for redemptiontoken-relations.xyz. However, it’s very new and specific – an OS would need to incorporate such features (or similar stablecoin liquidity pools) so that an end-user can seamlessly convert fund shares into a payment currency on demand. Likewise, direct wallet-to-wallet transfers of fund tokens have only recently been enabled in a limited way (Franklin’s platform now allows whitelisted wallet transfers of BENJI and even purchases via stablecoin instead of only via fiatthedefiant.io). A comprehensive solution is needed to handle whitelist management, compliance checks, and the mechanics of moving these tokens between internal wallets or to counterparties in a compliant manner.
	• Lack of Automated Yield “Sweeps” and Cash Optimization: In traditional corporate treasury, it’s common to have sweep accounts that automatically move excess cash into overnight investments and back to checking by morning. No analogous automation exists yet for on-chain treasuries. A finance team must manually decide how much to allocate to a tokenized fund and execute the transaction (and similarly plan redemptions when cash is needed). This manual approach is inefficient and prone to leaving idle cash uninvested. The current landscape lacks rule-based automation – e.g. “if wallet balance in USDC exceeds X, invest the remainder into Fund Y until needed” or “redeem from Fund by 4pm to meet next-day payroll”. Such capabilities would significantly enhance intraday yield capture but would require integrations between wallets, fund platforms, and possibly forecasting tools. No off-the-shelf software today offers this kind of programmable treasury allocation.
	• Treasury/Accounting Integration and Reporting: Corporate treasurers require robust reporting (daily positions, accrued interest, P&L, audit trails) and integration with ERP or TMS (Treasury Management System) software. Presently, holding on-chain funds can be an operational headache – finance teams might have to manually reconcile blockchain balances with accounting records, figure out how to mark-to-market these tokens, and track interest income from smart contract distributions. Traditional TMS providers (like Kyriba, SAP, etc.) are only beginning to explore digital asset connectivity. There is a capability gap in pulling on-chain data into familiar formats. For example, interest from BUIDL is paid daily in new tokens (like an auto-reinvest dividend)ccn.comccn.com, which accountants need to record correctly. A token treasury platform could fill this gap with features like automated reporting, CSV exports or APIs that feed into accounting systems, and perhaps policy controls to enforce internal limits (akin to how a TMS manages bank account signatories and limits).
	• User Experience and Security for Corporate Use: Managing crypto assets requires handling private keys or using custody solutions – something most corporate finance teams have little experience with. Current crypto wallet UX is not enterprise-friendly: firms either use retail wallets (with a single key – a non-starter for corporate controls) or they use custodian platforms like Fireblocks, which while secure, were initially built with crypto trading firms in mind rather than conservative treasurers. As one analysis noted, many mid-sized businesses find “no easy-to-use, affordable wallet solution” that provides multi-user access, permissions, and safe custody – offerings like Fireblocks and BitGo are powerful but costly and geared towards large institutionsblockeden.xyz. The result is that some firms simply avoid holding tokens directly because the operational burden and risk is too high. This tooling gap in enterprise wallet UX is crying out to be filledblockeden.xyzblockeden.xyz. A tokenized cash management platform would need built-in MPC or multi-signature wallets with role-based controls, an intuitive interface for non-crypto natives, and safeguards like address whitelisting, policy rules, and insurance – all packaged in a user experience similar to online banking (so that finance staff can use it with confidence).
In summary, while the assets (tokenized funds) have arrived, the infrastructure and software to utilize them easily is lagging behind. The current landscape lacks an end-to-end solution to: (a) aggregate and secure these assets across chains, (b) automate yield and liquidity actions, and (c) integrate with existing corporate processes. These missing capabilities represent an opening for a purpose-built platform.
4. Is There an Existing End-to-End Solution Today?
There appears to be a genuine shortage of integrated software for this specific use-case. No incumbent treasury system or crypto platform fully addresses the “tokenized MMF across wallets and payments” problem end-to-end as of 2025:
	• Traditional corporate TMS providers do not yet support digital asset management in a meaningful way. While some (like Kyriba) have announced partnerships to experiment with blockchain payments or stablecoins, these are limited add-ons. For example, Kyriba partnered with JPMorgan’s blockchain unit and others to allow blockchain deposit accounts and connectivity to crypto paymentsjpmorgan.comjpmorgan.com, but this covers using stablecoins for payments, not managing on-chain investments. No evidence suggests current TMS platforms can directly interface with tokenized fund subscriptions or track on-chain yields. Treasury portals like ICD or FiSIM (which corporations use to allocate among various money market funds) have decades of development but are focused on traditional funds and banks. They have only just begun to discuss tokenized funds as a new distribution categorycalastone.com. In short, legacy providers are not there yet.
	• On the crypto side, several infrastructure startups cover pieces of the puzzle but not the whole. Fireblocks is the leading digital asset custody/operations platform for institutions. It provides secure MPC wallets, a policy engine, and connectivity to exchanges/DeFifireblocks.comfireblocks.com. A treasury team could use Fireblocks as the backbone for securing keys and executing transactions. However, Fireblocks on its own is general-purpose: it’s not tailored to tokenized fund workflows (e.g. it doesn’t natively handle the Securitize onboarding or provide a UI for “invest in BlackRock fund” – the user would still have to manage those steps externally). Fireblocks also focuses heavily on trading, settlement and payments; while it touts “treasury management” capabilities, those revolve around safeguarding assets and moving value, rather than optimizing yield or integrating with fiat bankingfireblocks.comfireblocks.com. Utila (a newer startup) is closer to our use-case in that it brands itself as a “crypto treasury operations” platform. Utila offers an MPC wallet system with features for on/off-ramping, automated payments, and a unified dashboard for assetsutila.ioutila.io. Its initial market focus has been stablecoin operations – serving clients like payment providers and neo-banks who use stablecoins for cross-border flowscoindesk.comcoindesk.com. Utila’s platform handles things like multi-chain stablecoin custody, compliance checks, and conversion via exchanges. That addresses treasury payment needs for enterprises working with stablecoins, but it doesn’t yet explicitly cover managing tokenized MMF assets (e.g. no known integration with Securitize or fund tokens is advertised). Essentially, Utila solves the payment and wallet side for stablecoins, but not the investment side of sweeping into T-bills – a complementary capability the market is still missing. Other players include Coinbase Prime/Custody, Anchorage, and BitGo, which large institutions use for holding crypto. Like Fireblocks, these provide secure custody and in some cases access to staking or DeFi yields, but none offers a purpose-built solution for active cash management in tokenized funds.
	• Meanwhile, money market fund providers themselves have not released full-featured software for clients to use these tokens across wallets. BlackRock’s and Franklin’s offerings are accessed through fairly bespoke means: Franklin initially used its own Benji mobile app for retail and now works with platforms like Securitize and DBS Bank’s exchange for distributiontoken-relations.xyz. BlackRock’s BUIDL is distributed via Securitize and likely through private banking channels. These are point solutions, not a consolidated tool a corporate treasury could adopt for multi-fund management. In fact, Securitize’s recent moves (like enabling direct stablecoin swaps for fund shares) suggest that even the issuers recognize the need for better infrastructuretoken-relations.xyztoken-relations.xyz. But Securitize’s portal alone is only for the assets it supports and focuses on compliance/issuance; it’s not a full treasury ops platform for the client.
All told, no single product today spans the entire workflow – from KYC onboarding to token custody, to automated yield allocation, to instant stablecoin liquidity and reporting. If a corporate or crypto-native firm wanted to do this end-to-end, they’d have to cobble together multiple solutions: e.g. use Securitize (or a broker) to buy fund tokens, hold them in Fireblocks for safekeeping, manually swap or redeem via OTC desks when cash is needed, and keep records by extracting data from Etherscan and emails. This patchwork is cumbersome. The absence of a one-stop SaaS solution is affirmed by market research: in a 2025 forum, asset managers noted that **80% of digital asset investors want to invest in tokenized MMFs, but only 25% currently do – partly because “too few options” and supporting services are availablecalastone.com. In other words, demand is pent-up, but the tooling and access are lagging, creating friction.
The shortage is real: while certain platforms address parts of the need (Fireblocks for security, Utila for stablecoin treasury, exchange networks for liquidity), none provide the holistic “cash management OS” that an institutional client would want for operating with tokenized money funds on a daily basis. This gap represents the opportunity.
5. Competitive Landscape: Who’s Trying and Where They Fall Short
Given the nascent nature of this space, direct competitors attempting a “Tokenized Treasury OS” are few, but adjacent players can be considered partial competitors. Here’s the landscape and their limitations:
	• Fireblocks and Custodial Platforms: Fireblocks is often the default choice for any institution handling digital assets, and it markets a crypto treasury management solution broadlyfireblocks.com. Its strengths are top-tier security (MPC, hardware isolation, security certifications) and a robust policy/gov­er­nance engine for transaction approvalsfireblocks.comfireblocks.com. It also has a network of exchanges and counterparties integrated for quick transfersfireblocks.com. However, Fireblocks is not specialized for yield management. It doesn’t proactively manage balances or connect to traditional MMFs. A corporate using Fireblocks would still need significant internal development or processes to utilize tokenized funds – Fireblocks would securely store the tokens and could connect to an exchange to trade RLUSD or USDC, but it won’t tell you when to redeem, how much to invest, or handle fund-specific compliance. Additionally, Fireblocks’ cost and complexity can be a barrier for all but the largest firms (many smaller crypto companies find it expensive and instead take on more risk by using simpler solutionsblockeden.xyz). Anchorage (a federally chartered digital asset bank) and Coinbase Custody/Prime similarly focus on custody and trade execution. Coinbase has taken steps toward crypto treasury services (for example, Coinbase partnered with BlackRock to let Aladdin platform users see Coinbase-held crypto in their portfolio), but that’s more about portfolio visibility than active treasury operations. None of these custodians provide the full cash management workflows or integration to fiat payments that a true Treasury OS would require.
	• Stablecoin Payment Networks and Fintechs: A wave of payment-focused players is tackling the problem of moving stablecoins around for institutions. For instance, Fireblocks recently launched a stablecoin payments network with 40+ institutions, enabling instant transfers of stablecoins among membersfinance.yahoo.com. Notably, Stripe’s acquisition of stablecoin startup Bridge in 2024, and Circle’s anticipated IPO, were seen as watershed events accelerating stablecoin integration into mainstream financecoindesk.comcoindesk.com. The result is that companies like Stripe (via Bridge) aim to let businesses send/receive stablecoins and have them auto-convert to local currency in bank accountsblockeden.xyz. These developments address one side of the coin: payments and off-ramps. They ensure that if a firm wants to pay a $5M invoice in stablecoin or pull stablecoin receipts into fiat, it’s becoming easier and fasterblockeden.xyzblockeden.xyz. However, these solutions generally do not address the investment/yield side of treasury. A company might use Stripe/Bridge to handle payouts and conversions, but Stripe is not going to invest their spare cash into T-bills for them. That remains a gap.
	• Crypto-Native Treasury Startups: We discussed Utila in the previous section – it is one of the only startups explicitly branding around “crypto treasury operations”. Utila’s focus is on providing a unified platform for businesses to hold and use digital assets with compliance. It has gained traction with fintech and payment companies, highlighting features like on/off ramp, automated payouts, and a consolidated dashboard for stablecoin liquidityutila.iocoindesk.com. Where Utila falls short is in yield integration: it does not (yet) offer built-in access to tokenized MMFs or other yield-bearing instruments for treasury. Their roadmap (from press releases) shows attention to stablecoin infrastructure and staking (e.g. partnering with Coinchange to offer some yield products, and enabling staking via Yield.xyz)utila.ioutila.io. This suggests they recognize clients want returns on idle assets, but likely via crypto-native yields. A true competitor would be one that explicitly connects to RWA token funds – currently Utila doesn’t advertise that. Another crypto-native tool is Request Finance, which helps companies manage crypto invoicing, payroll, and expenses (with stablecoins), but again, that’s operational finance, not investment management.
	• Money Market Fund Portals & Banks: As mentioned, traditional portals like ICD, SunGard/FIS, or bank-owned portals (J.P. Morgan’s, BlackRock’s Cash portal, etc.) may evolve to include tokenized funds. In fact, Calastone (a fund infrastructure provider) notes that fresh competition is emerging around the “portal experience” as MMF offerings proliferatecalastone.comcalastone.com. BlackRock’s launch of a tokenized MMF is cited as an example that requires new distribution networks to access the range of productscalastone.com. We can expect large asset managers and banks to adapt their tech eventually, but right now, these portals likely treat the tokenized funds as just another fund on a list (if they include them at all). They also usually don’t handle crypto wallets or stablecoins – they operate in the traditional manner (bank wires in/out of funds). A corporate could invest in BlackRock’s BUIDL through a bank portal if the bank offers it, but that investment might actually be processed off-chain (the bank aggregates and BlackRock still issues tokens to some custodian’s wallet). Without native blockchain integrations, those portals won’t empower treasurers to move tokens between self-custody wallets or use them as collateral elsewhere. In short, incumbents are starting to notice the tokenized fund trend, but their solutions aren’t Web3-enabled.
	• Emerging RWA Integration Efforts: We should note initiatives like the Ripple – Franklin Templeton – DBS Bank collaboration: DBS (a major bank in Singapore) integrated Franklin’s on-chain fund (BENJI) and Ripple’s stablecoin (RLUSD) into its digital exchange, allowing clients to trade between RLUSD and BENJI tokens and even planning to let BENJI be used as loan collateral for credit linestoken-relations.xyztoken-relations.xyz. This is a compelling use-case (using tokenized T-bills as collateral to borrow cash), and DBS had to custom-build it. This hints at competition from integrated finance platforms – perhaps tomorrow other large banks or exchanges will create their own portals for tokenized assets. But these will likely be closed ecosystems (DBS’s solution is for its own clients). A startup can differentiate by being network-agnostic and client-agnostic, i.e. serving any institution and aggregating across many fund providers and banks.
In summary, the competition is currently fragmented. Each group – custodians, payment networks, treasury fintechs, traditional portals – addresses a slice (security, liquidity, usability, distribution respectively), but no one has “solved” the holistic tokenized treasury management use-case yet. Our proposed platform can occupy this white space. Competitors will inevitably converge here (Fireblocks could extend into automated yield, Kyriba could add digital asset support, Securitize could build more user-facing tools), but as of now they “fall short” either by scope (only handling stablecoins or custody) or by openness (closed bank-specific solutions). This new venture can establish a lead by being the first purpose-built SaaS for institutional on-chain cash management, while partnering where needed (rather than reinventing custody or liquidity networks from scratch).
6. High-Level Product Roadmap and Key Integrations
Building a Tokenized Treasury/Cash Management OS will require a phased approach. Below is a high-level roadmap with major milestones and integrations:
	• Phase 1: MVP – Secure Wallet and Basic Fund Access (0–6 months). The initial product must solve immediate pain points around secure custody and visibility of tokenized cash assets. This likely means integrating with a proven custody solution (for instance, leveraging Fireblocks’ MPC wallets via API, or another MPC library) to provide each client with a secure vault for holding stablecoins and fund tokens. On top of this, develop a dashboard UI where a treasurer can see all their balances across supported assets and networks in real time. Key integrations in this phase:
		○ Fund onboarding APIs: Work with transfer agents like Securitize (who manage BUIDL, VBILL, etc.) to integrate the KYC/whitelisting process. Ideally, the user could complete compliance requirements for a fund within our platform or be directed in-app. Even if not fully automated, the platform can guide the user through onboarding with each issuer.
		○ Core fund transactions: Implement the ability to initiate subscriptions and redemptions for at least one or two flagship tokenized funds (e.g. BlackRock BUIDL and Franklin BENJI). This involves integration with whatever interface the fund provides – possibly a REST API or even sending instructions to the transfer agent. At MVP, this might be semi-manual (e.g. the platform generates the required forms or transaction instructions and notifies the user when to send USDC or receive tokens). The goal is to prove we can get a client’s money into and out of a fund efficiently.
		○ Basic stablecoin ramp: Integrate with a major stablecoin on/off-ramp for fiat (such as Circle’s APIs for USDC issuance/redemption, or an exchange) so that users can convert fiat to USDC (or vice versa) as part of funding these investments. This ensures the platform covers the flow from a client’s bank to on-chain and back.
Milestone: Onboarding a pilot corporate (or crypto fund) that uses the platform to move, say, $10M into a tokenized T-bill fund and back to fiat or stablecoin successfully, all with appropriate approvals and security in place.
	• Phase 2: Automation and Yield Optimization (6–18 months). Once the basics are in place, the next stage is to implement the smart workflows and integrations that truly differentiate the product:
		○ Automated Sweeps & Rules Engine: Develop a feature for users to define rules/policies (e.g. “Keep $1M in wallet X for operational needs, invest excess into Fund Y daily at 5pm” or “Maintain at least $500k in USDC liquidity, otherwise redeem from Fund when needed”). This likely requires scheduling capability, monitoring wallet balances, and programmatically executing fund transactions. Integration with calendar and cut-off times for funds is needed (though with RLUSD-like instant swaps, cut-off may be moot). Essentially, bring the convenience of bank sweep accounts to on-chain treasury.
		○ Real-Time Stablecoin Liquidity: By this phase, integrate deeper liquidity solutions. For example, integrate with the RLUSD stablecoin exchange mechanism that Securitize/Ripple set uptoken-relations.xyztoken-relations.xyz – so that our users can swap fund shares for RLUSD (or USDC) in-app instantly. Also, connect to major DeFi lending protocols or prime brokers to allow borrowing against fund tokens if the user desires (much like DBS is exploring repo-style lending with BENJI as collateraltoken-relations.xyz). This could involve integration with a platform like Maple or Aave Arc (if they list such RWAs), or partnering with a bank/credit provider to handle that off-chain.
		○ Accounting and Analytics: Build out the reporting engine – give users downloadable reports of interest earned, mark-to-market valuations, and perhaps a forecast tool. Integration with accounting software (even if just QuickBooks or Netsuite connectors initially) would start here. Also incorporate performance analytics: e.g., show cumulative dividends earned (BUIDL paid out ~$7M in dividends in its first monthsccn.comccn.com; our platform should track and display such income for the user’s holdings). Compliance logging (audit trail of who approved which transfer, etc.) would be added to satisfy auditors and internal control.
		○ Additional Fund Integrations: Expand the menu of supported yield sources. By mid-2025, there will be multiple tokenized Treasury products (BlackRock, Franklin, VanEck, Ondo Finance’s OUSG/USDY, possibly Galaxy, Hashnote, etc.). We would integrate as many as are relevant so clients can diversify issuers or maturities. Each may have its own onboarding and tech interface – this phase is heavy on integrating those different APIs or processes into one seamless user flow.
Milestone: The platform can fully automate a treasury operation like: end-of-day, move all surplus cash from a Coinbase Commerce account (say in USDC) into two tokenized funds, then next morning redeem a portion to pay salaries through the bank – all without manual intervention beyond initial rule setup. Also, onboard a second cohort of clients (e.g. a fintech firm and a DAO or family office) to prove broader demand and refine user experience.
	• Phase 3: Ecosystem and Partnerships (18–36 months). In this stage, the product should mature into a comprehensive OS that plugs into the wider financial ecosystem:
		○ Enterprise Integrations: Partner with one or more traditional TMS providers or ERP systems. For example, build an integration with Kyriba or SAP’s treasury module so that mutual clients can view and initiate blockchain treasury ops from their existing dashboards. This could involve exposing our platform’s capabilities via API or even white-labeling in some cases. The idea is to meet larger corporates where they already operate, accelerating adoption among conservative players.
		○ Bank and Payment Network Partnerships: By this time, major banks might be interested in offering our solution to their corporate clients, or at least hooking into it. We could integrate with bank APIs (via open banking or host-to-host connections) to initiate fiat wires corresponding to token transactions. For example, if a user redeems $5M from a fund to a bank account, our platform could automatically trigger a pre-set wire template at the user’s bank. Also, joining forces with networks like SWIFT (which is piloting tokenized asset connectivity) or Visa’s stablecoin initiatives could provide more seamless off-ramps. Essentially, become the glue between crypto liquidity and traditional accounts.
		○ Global and Multi-Currency Support: Add support for non-USD stablecoins and funds. If EUR, GBP, or other currency T-bill tokens emerge (or even tokenized bank deposits), incorporate those. This will appeal to multi-national corporates who want to manage cash in different currencies on-chain.
		○ Regulatory Licensing if needed: As the business scales, evaluate obtaining licenses such as a broker-dealer or RIA (Registered Investment Advisor) if we move into handling client assets directly or giving explicit investment advice. Up to Phase 2, we might avoid holding custody ourselves (partnering for that) and avoid discretionary management to stay purely SaaS. But in Phase 3, to integrate deeply with banks or handle pooled transactions, some regulatory compliance steps (e.g. becoming an SEC-registered transfer agent or broker for digital securities) might be considered to solidify credibility and trust.
		○ AI and Intelligent Treasury (optional nice-to-have): Incorporate analytics that, for example, suggest optimal allocations or forecast interest based on yield curves. This is more speculative, but a differentiator in the long run (imagine an AI assistant that tells the treasurer “you have excess cash that could earn an extra $50k this week if moved into Fund X, click here to approve”).
Milestone: Secure a marquee partnership – e.g. a large bank reselling our platform to its clients, or a TMS company integrating us as their “digital asset module”. Also by this time, have a stable revenue stream from a diverse client base, and possibly regulatory clearance to operate in key jurisdictions (US, EU, Singapore, etc.).
Throughout these phases, user feedback, security, and compliance will be continuous threads. Each integration (whether with Securitize, Circle, an exchange, or a bank API) will require robust testing and likely legal review (to ensure handling of assets complies with fund prospectus rules and KYC/AML laws). The roadmap above is ambitious, but hitting even the Phase 1 and 2 capabilities would establish a strong first-mover advantage.
7. Target Customer Segments and Use Cases
The potential customer base spans both crypto-native firms and forward-thinking traditional enterprises. Key segments that would derive clear value – and thus likely pay for – this platform include:
	• Crypto Exchanges and Trading Firms: These businesses (centralized exchanges, market makers, OTC desks) often hold large inventories of crypto and fiat for operational liquidity. Instead of leaving dollars in bank accounts or stablecoins idle on exchange, they could use tokenized T-bills to earn yield on customer reserves or corporate funds. For example, an exchange that has $100M in USD as buffer could easily park a portion in a daily-liquid fund via our OS and improve its interest income. Such firms value automation (to sweep funds in/out without affecting 24/7 exchange operations) and security – both of which our platform provides.
	• Stablecoin Issuers and Crypto Yield Platforms: A stablecoin issuer (especially smaller or non-bank issuers) might use the platform internally to manage its reserve portfolio on-chain. While large issuers like Circle work directly with BlackRock, a smaller issuer or an algorithmic stable project could leverage tokenized MMFs as reserves (the GENIUS Act now explicitly allows stablecoin reserves to be in government MMFsdechert.com). Similarly, DeFi platforms or custodians that promise clients safe yield (e.g. “earn on your stablecoins”) could integrate our OS as the backend to deploy user funds into regulated yield-bearing tokens rather than riskier lending. They become distribution partners in a sense, but also end-users of the tech.
	• Crypto Treasuries of Web3 Projects and DAOs: Many crypto projects raised capital (in fiat or stablecoins) and need to manage that treasury. Traditionally they either kept it in crypto (risky) or in stablecoins (no yield). An increasing number of DAO treasuries and foundation treasuries are interested in RWAs to preserve and grow their funds safely. Our platform could serve as an institutional-grade solution for DAOs or foundations, especially if they are willing to establish an entity to pass KYC. They would benefit from professional treasury management (some DAOs even hire outside managers for this purpose). The OS would let them deploy funds into T-bills easily and document the activity transparently (useful for community reporting).
	• Fintechs, Payment Companies, and Neo-banks: These are companies that straddle crypto and traditional finance – for example, cross-border payment startups, remittance apps, or neo-banks that offer crypto wallets. As noted, many such firms are already heavy stablecoin users (for speed and global reach). They have “float” or working capital that flows through their systems. By using our platform, they could earn intraday interest on float balances. A payments provider in Latin America or Africa, for instance, might hold USDC for a day or two while settling between currencies – plugging into a tokenized fund during that window could generate extra revenue. The Coindesk report on Utila mentioned payment providers and neobanks are actively seeking stablecoin infrastructurecoindesk.comcoindesk.com, which implies these firms would also desire yield on those stablecoins if available.
	• Corporate Treasuries of Tech and Multinational Companies: This is the holy grail – getting Fortune 500 or large private companies to use the platform. Realistically, most corporate CFOs are conservative and will be slower to adopt on-chain solutions. But certain profiles of companies could be early: Tech companies and high-cash startups (who understand crypto and might already hold crypto on balance sheet) or companies operating in markets with weak banking (that have turned to stablecoins out of necessity). For example, a global manufacturing firm that does business in emerging markets could use stablecoins to bypass slow correspondent banking, and with our tool, they could also keep that money in a yield-bearing form until deployment. These customers would emphasize integration with their existing processes and regulatory compliance. They also might insist on a reputable custodian integration (which we have) and perhaps insurance on the assets. If we achieve that, the value proposition of a “secure, liquid, interest-earning global treasury” could attract them.
	• Investment Funds, Family Offices, and Asset Managers: Hedge funds that trade crypto, crypto venture funds between investments, or family offices with an appetite for alternative yields could use the OS to manage their cash. For instance, a crypto VC fund might keep some dry powder in USDC waiting for opportunities – rather than sitting idly, they could earn 5% in a tokenized Treasury fund via our platform until they deploy capital. Family offices, which often seek higher yields on cash, might find the regulated nature of tokenized MMFs appealing (as opposed to, say, DeFi yield farms). Even traditional asset managers (who manage money market funds themselves) might use the platform for their internal treasury or to test the waters in crypto. In fact, 80% of “digital treasurers” expressed interest in tokenized fundscalastone.com, indicating various types of institutions are looking at this.
	• Stablecoin-Focused Banks or Trusts: An emerging category is banks or trusts that facilitate stablecoin transactions (e.g. Anchorage Digital Bank, or Silvergate (formerly), Signature’s Signet platform, etc.). These institutions could partner or use our OS to offer clients an option like “sweep your Signet balance into T-bills overnight”. Essentially, they’d embed our capabilities into their client offerings. They might not be direct paying customers (if it’s a partnership model), but they represent an end-user base (their corporate clients) that we could indirectly serve.
To summarize, target customers fall into two broad groups: (a) Crypto-native firms (exchanges, crypto funds, Web3 companies) that already operate in digital assets and need better cash management, and (b) Traditional corporates/fintechs that are starting to use stablecoins or want to optimize large cash positions. Both groups stand to gain significant efficiency and yield via the platform. Initially, we expect crypto-native and fintech clients to adopt first (as they have fewer internal hurdles). Over time, as the product proves itself and regulatory clarity improves, larger corporates and financial institutions can come onboard. The potential revenue per client in latter group is high (they manage huge cash balances), so capturing even a few marquee corporate treasuries would be a big win.
8. Market Timing: Why the Tooling Gap Exists and How Long It May Last
The current tooling gap exists because the market moved faster than the infrastructure. Tokenized money market funds leapt from concept to billions in AUM within ~1 year (2024–2025)ccn.comcalastone.com, catching many service providers off guard. There are a few key reasons for this lag:
	• Historical Lack of Demand/Use-Case: Before 2023, interest rates were near zero and few reputable institutions were involved in on-chain finance. There was little demand from corporate treasurers to put cash on blockchain, since it offered no yield advantage and carried regulatory uncertainty. The focus in crypto was on speculative trading, not cash management. Thus, treasury software vendors and fintechs didn’t prioritize this niche. As one report aptly paraphrased Gibson, “the future is here, it’s just not evenly distributed” – the tokenization future arrived, but the tools are only now catching upcalastone.com.
	• Specialized Knowledge Required: A tokenized treasury OS sits at the intersection of traditional finance, blockchain tech, and enterprise software. It requires understanding money market funds regulations, crypto wallets, settlement mechanics, and corporate controls – a rare combination. Many crypto startups lacked treasury domain knowledge, and traditional fintechs lacked crypto expertise. This led to a situation where no incumbent had both the incentive and capability to fill the gap immediately. We’re basically seeing an infrastructure lag that often follows a new financial innovation. For example, stablecoins existed since 2014, but only now in 2025 are we seeing robust enterprise stablecoin payment networks formfinance.yahoo.comfortune.com. Tokenized funds just hit critical mass in 2024–2025, so we are likely 1–2 years away from seeing mature tooling for them – unless a focused effort (like our venture) accelerates it.
	• Regulatory Caution and Uncertainty: Until recently, players may have held back due to unclear regulations around digital securities and stablecoins. The GENIUS Act of 2025 clarified many issues (stablecoins not being treated as securities, who can issue, etc.)dechert.comdechert.com. Similarly, the SEC’s growing comfort (e.g. approving certain blockchain-based fund processes) and the President’s Working Group recommendations in 2025dechert.com have reduced the fear of building in this space. This new clarity likely means the next 12–18 months will see rapid development of tokenization infrastructure. But up until now, firms were hesitant to invest heavily in tools that might have run afoul of regulators or found no market if regulations turned unfavorable. Now that the logjam is broken, we have a window where being first confers a big advantage.
Given these factors, how long will the gap persist? It’s a race. The industry dialogue (as evidenced by forums and blogs) suggests the gap will be filled – the question is by whom and when. Calastone’s research expects “within 18 months, a significant number of asset managers will have launched or be developing tokenized MMFs”calastone.com. This means more assets and more competition for distribution, likely drawing attention from big fintech and banks to provide support tools. We can also expect existing treasury tech providers to add basic support in the next 1–2 years (for example, by late 2026 Kyriba or FIS might announce some blockchain asset integration, and banks like JPMorgan could extend their portals). However, large incumbents typically move deliberately – doing pilots, ensuring regulatory sign-off, etc. Meanwhile, crypto infrastructure startups are iterating quickly but tend to focus on the largest immediate markets (trading, custody, payments), not niche treasury needs. This creates an estimated 12–24 month sweet spot where a dedicated solution can establish itself without facing a direct full-feature rival.
After perhaps 2–3 years, if tokenized assets continue to grow toward a “multi-trillion dollar market” (as some predict for the coming years)token-relations.xyztoken-relations.xyz, then certainly big players will enter. We might see a convergence by 2027: for instance, a large custodian might acquire a startup (like Stripe did with Bridge) to offer an integrated service, or asset managers might build their own client-facing portals with capabilities similar to what we envision. The good news for our venture is that even when they do, they’ll likely focus on their slice (e.g. a BlackRock app for BlackRock funds). Corporates managing multi-issuer portfolios or wanting neutral platforms will still seek independent solutions. Also, by being early, we can accumulate domain knowledge, client trust, and possibly network effects (e.g. relationships with most fund issuers) that make it hard to rip and replace.
In summary, the market’s tooling gap exists because the innovation (tokenized yield) outran the infrastructure, and it will persist until specialized or incumbent solutions catch up. We gauge we have about 1–2 years of runway to become the go-to platform before significant competition arises. Even then, given the conservatism in corporate treasury, the market likely won’t fully catch up overnight. It’s plausible to have a 3–5 year window of growth before this space commoditizes. And if we execute well, by the time others catch up, we could be the established leader (or an acquisition target for an incumbent that decides not to build in-house).
9. Revenue Model and Outlook
Monetizing a SaaS platform for institutional treasury management can follow proven B2B fintech patterns, with adjustments for the crypto context. Here’s how we envision earning revenue, the timeline, and payment mix:
	• SaaS Subscription Fees: The primary model would be a tiered subscription for the platform. Clients could be charged based on AUM under management, number of users, and feature tiers. For example, a base tier might allow managing up to $50M in assets with standard support, while higher tiers cover larger AUM and include premium features (custom integrations, white-glove support, etc.). This is analogous to how both treasury software and crypto custody providers price: e.g. Fireblocks reportedly has annual contracts in the tens or hundreds of thousands of dollars for enterprise clients (reflecting value and security). We would calibrate our fees to provide clear ROI – if we’re helping a firm earn an extra 4-5% on idle cash, a subscription equal to a small fraction of that gain is justifiable. When can this start? Potentially as soon as we have a functional MVP with real money flows (perhaps 6-12 months in). Early pilot customers might get discounts or free trials, but by Year 2 we’d aim to convert them to paying status as we demonstrate value.
	• Transaction/Usage Fees: In addition to flat subscription, we can earn usage-based revenue on certain activities:
		○ Trade/Conversion Fees: Whenever a client converts one asset to another (e.g. swapping fund tokens to stablecoin, or vice versa), we could take a small bps fee or spread. This is similar to how crypto exchanges or prime brokers charge trading fees, or how money market portals sometimes get a distribution fee from fund providers. For instance, if a client swaps $10M of BUIDL to RLUSD via our integration, a 0.01% fee would be $1,000 – relatively negligible to the client (given the yield benefit they got) but meaningful at scale for us. We’d have to be transparent and competitive in these fees (perhaps bundling a certain volume free in the subscription and then tiered).
		○ Assets Under Management Fee: Another approach could be charging an AUM-based fee (like 5–10 bps annually on assets managed through the platform). This is akin to an advisory fee. However, many corporate clients will balk at sharing yield in that way, and it starts to make us look like an asset manager (with potential regulatory issues). A possible middle ground: we might get referral fees from the fund issuers (some tokenized funds might pay platforms for bringing deposits, similar to how traditional money funds have distribution/shareholder servicing fees). If such arrangements exist, that could supplement revenue without directly taxing the user.
		○ Premium Services: If we facilitate credit or lending using the assets (say we integrate with a bank to offer credit lines against T-bill tokens), we could take an origination or arrangement fee from those deals. Those are more one-off and relationship-based, but it’s a future angle especially if we become a hub for such financing.
	• Timeline for Revenue Ramp: We expect a relatively long sales cycle for big clients (treasury decisions are not quick), but also sticky recurring revenue once onboarded. Year 1 (2025, assuming launch) might involve a handful of pilot customers, possibly paying nominal or at-cost fees. By Year 2, as reference cases emerge, we could start signing more institutions onto annual contracts. The interest rate environment will drive urgency: as long as yields are high (4-5%), the incentive to adopt is high – and thus we could see significant uptake within 1-2 years. If, hypothetically, interest rates start falling in late 2025 or 2026, it could slightly reduce urgency, but by then many may have embedded the practice into policy.
In terms of magnitude, the market is large: U.S. corporates held ~$7.2 trillion in money market funds in 2022calastone.com. We don’t need much of that on-chain to build revenue. If, say, $20B of tokenized cash is managed via platforms like ours by 2027 (a speculative but feasible scenario given trendscalastone.com), a 5 bps fee on that would be $10M annually. Add subscriptions and other fees, and we could be looking at a healthy eight-figure revenue business in a few years. Initially, though, success will be measured by getting even a small number of high-value clients (each client could be five to six figures ARR).
	• Fiat vs Crypto Payment for Services: We should be flexible. Many institutional clients will prefer to pay our subscription in fiat (USD/EUR) via invoice, as they do with any software vendor. We’ll accommodate that for their convenience and for our own cash flow stability. However, given we operate in the crypto sphere, we can also accept payments in stablecoins or crypto. In fact, some crypto-native customers might want to pay in USDC or USDT (since that might be their functional currency). There’s also a nice symmetry if a client can pay us out of the yield they earned via the platform – for example, a client could allocate a tiny portion of their monthly BENJI dividends to pay our fee in USDC. We’ll likely see a mix: crypto companies paying in crypto, traditional companies paying in fiat. Over time, as stablecoins get more mainstream and regulatory clarity (thanks to GENIUS Act) draws more usage, we might see even traditional firms comfortable paying in a USD stablecoin. So we should build infrastructure to accept both. For forecasting, we can treat all revenue in dollar-equivalents (and perhaps hold mostly fiat ourselves to manage our own risk).
One noteworthy aspect: Because this is a B2B SaaS, revenue will be relatively lumpy and dependent on client acquisition – but churn should be low if we integrate deeply. Also, unlike retail DeFi, we won’t rely on token incentives or crypto hype; revenue will come from straightforward fees for service. This makes the business model understandable to investors and easier to forecast (provided we can sign on enterprise clients).
In conclusion, we expect to start monetizing within the first 12 months of product launch (even if modestly), and scaling revenue as we add clients and possibly assets-based fees. The combination of subscription + transaction fees gives us a diversified stream: subscription ensures a baseline recurring revenue, while transaction/AUM fees scale with client usage. Importantly, our value proposition is directly tied to financial gains (yield) for the client, making it easier to justify our fees – a true win-win, we make money when they make money.
10. Key Risks, Infrastructure Considerations, and Investor Concerns
As with any ambitious venture at the intersection of new finance and old, there are several risks and concerns to address upfront:
	• Regulatory/Compliance Risk: This is perhaps the largest question mark. We are dealing with tokenized shares of funds (which are effectively securities) and facilitating their movement. We must ensure that our platform’s activities (and our company’s role) do not trigger regulatory requirements that we aren’t prepared for. For example, if we start actively brokering trades of fund tokens or pooling client orders, we might need a Broker-Dealer license or ATS (Alternative Trading System) registration in the U.S. However, if we position ourselves as a software provider (SaaS) and not an intermediary party to transactions, we can mitigate this. In practice, we’d likely structure so that the client is always the legal transacting party with the fund issuer, and we’re just providing tech and routing instructions (similar to how a Bloomberg Terminal can send trade orders but Bloomberg isn’t the broker). We will also enforce that only eligible, KYC’d users access the fund investments through our platform, in line with the transfer agents’ requirements, effectively extending the compliance of those issuers. Still, investors will rightly ask: What if regulations tighten or change? One scenario is that regulators might impose stricter rules on digital asset custody or stablecoin use – our reliance on third-party custodians and a stablecoin off-ramp like RLUSDccn.comccn.com actually helps, because those partners (Anchorage/Fireblocks, Ripple/Securitize for RLUSD) are themselves regulated entities or working within regulatory frameworks. Our job is to stay agile – e.g., if the SEC were to require tokenized fund platforms to register, we’d be ready to do so or partner with someone who is. Compliance will be a selling point, not just a risk – clients will want assurance that using our platform won’t get them in hot water. Therefore, we’ll invest in strong legal counsel and possibly advisory board members from the regulatory world.
	• Security and Custody Risk: We are dealing with potentially large sums of money in digital form. Any security breach or loss of keys could be catastrophic not only for clients but for our reputation. We mitigate this by leveraging battle-tested custody tech (don’t roll our own crypto custody if we don’t have to). By integrating with top-tier providers (Fireblocks, BitGo, etc.), we inherit their security protocols (MPC, HSMs, whitelisting, etc.). We also need rigorous internal controls – e.g. employees should never have the ability to unilaterally transfer client assets; our system should enforce multi-sig and client approvals. Offering insurance or bonding against losses could be necessary to win over conservative clients – we might obtain an insurance policy covering digital asset theft, which some custodians offer up to certain limits. Another vector is smart contract risk: if we use on-chain contracts (for automated swaps or perhaps yield aggregation), bugs could be exploited. We will likely rely on audited contracts from our partners (like the RLUSD swap contract created by Securitize/Ripple, which presumably was audited). We’ll also keep our own on-chain footprint minimal initially (lots of logic can be off-chain in our servers orchestrating calls to others). Investors will want to know our security posture – we should aim for certifications (ISO 27001, SOC2) as we mature, to demonstrate our commitment to safeguarding data and assets.
	• Reliance on Third-Party Infrastructure: Our value prop partially relies on the continued success and openness of the tokenized funds themselves and their facilitators. If, say, BlackRock decided to restrict access or only allow direct clients through Aladdin, that could hurt us. Or if Securitize (who’s heavily involved in many of these issuancesccn.comccn.com) has technical issues or changes APIs, we have to adapt. There’s also network risk: many tokenized funds use Solana, Polygon, etc. If those networks experience downtime or attacks, our platform and clients are impacted. We should design to be blockchain-agnostic to some extent (support multiple networks and ideally be able to migrate if one chain becomes problematic – e.g., if Solana had an outage, perhaps clients could use the Ethereum instance of a token via Wormhole). The liquidity of the tokens is another concern – if secondary market or swap liquidity dries up, clients might get stuck. We plan to integrate multiple liquidity options (direct redemption, RLUSD swap, even DEX or CEX markets if available) to avoid single points of failure. Essentially, our platform should be the smart routing layer that finds a way to get the client’s money where it needs to be, even if one path is blocked.
	• Market and Interest Rate Risk: Our opportunity is somewhat tied to the interest rate cycle. If in two years rates plunge back to zero, the appeal of tokenized T-bills might diminish (because holding stablecoin cash yields ~0 would be less painful). That said, even at zero rates, the efficiency and transparency of on-chain finance still hold benefits, but it’s a tougher sell if the financial incentive evaporates. We can partially hedge this risk by supporting whatever the best yield instruments are in any environment (if rates drop, maybe other assets get tokenized, like corporate bonds or bank deposits with yield – we could integrate those). Also, intraday liquidity benefits (faster settlement, 24/7 access) remain valuable regardless of rate, especially for global operations. But realistically, our adoption will accelerate faster in a high-rate environment. Investor concern: “Are you a business that only thrives when interest rates are high?” We should position ourselves as a longer-term infrastructure play – rates are cyclical, and even if they dip, the secular trend is tokenization and real-time finance, which isn’t going away. Plus, if we have a solid installed base, even in low-rate times, we could generate revenue as a critical ops platform (maybe focusing more on cost-saving and efficiency benefits then).
	• Adoption and Behavior Change: Convincing corporate treasurers to use a new system is non-trivial. There’s inertia and trust to overcome. We identified likely early adopters, but it still requires education and perhaps a champion in each organization to push it. The risk is a long sales cycle and slower adoption than expected. To mitigate, we might start with crypto-native companies (shorter sales cycle) and accumulate success stories to show traditional firms. Another approach is ensuring we meet enterprises halfway – integrate with their current banks or software as discussed, so it feels like an extension of what they already do, not a whole new workflow. Providing strong customer support, training, and perhaps guarantees (like a trial period where they can evaluate results risk-free) will help win them over. We should also be prepared for some companies to insist on specific needs (e.g. on-premise deployment or extra compliance features) – being flexible for key clients could be critical, even if it diverges from a pure multi-tenant SaaS model.
	• Competition and “Build vs Buy”: Another investor concern: what if big players just build this themselves quickly? For instance, could BlackRock or JPMorgan launch their own treasury management dApp that nullifies your value? Our defense: as noted, big firms move slower and often prefer to partner or buy proven solutions rather than build from scratch, especially for a highly technical blockchain product outside their main expertise. By the time they seriously consider it, we aim to have traction. Also, we serve a neutral role – a JPMorgan might build something for their clients, but other banks’ clients won’t use JPM’s tool. We can be the Switzerland that works across funds and banks. That said, we must keep innovating and possibly raise enough capital to stay ahead in product depth and integrations. The network effect of connecting many issuers, many wallets, and many payment networks can become a moat (a competitor would have to replicate all those connections). Patents could be a minor factor – e.g. Franklin has a patent pending on intraday yield techthedefiant.io; if we ever develop unique tech (say for allocating yield by the second across wallets), maybe we consider filings. But speed and customer love are better moats in this space than patents.
	• Operational and Talent Risks: As a startup providing financial infrastructure, we need a high-caliber team (blockchain devs, security engineers, finance experts). Talent is competitive and costly. We also will be operating 24/7 (since markets are 24/7), so we need robust DevOps and possibly global support to respond to any incident at any time. Ensuring platform uptime and correctness will be key – a single major error (like misrouting a transaction or an outage during a critical market event) could harm our reputation. We should implement rigorous testing (especially around edge cases like leap seconds for interest calc, chain forks, etc.) and have redundancy (maybe multi-cloud, backup nodes for blockchains, etc.). These are typical infra concerns but heightened here because we’re handling money.
In addressing investor concerns, we’d emphasize that the upside of this opportunity outweighs the manageable risks. The market timing is ideal, and we’re plugging an urgent gap that clients are actively looking to fill (e.g., anecdotal evidence of treasurers wanting these solutions now, given 5% yield disparitybacked.fi). We have multiple ways to mitigate risks: working with regulated partners, insurance, phasing our rollout to iron out issues with smaller clients before scaling to bigger ones, etc.
Ultimately, our goal is to demonstrate that we can create a secure, compliant, and indispensable piece of financial infrastructure for the tokenization era. By proactively tackling the risks above, we aim to inspire confidence that this venture can capture the opportunity responsibly and profitably.

